# Staged Linux SDK package (scripts/build-linux-sdk.sh output)
dist/

# V8 archives for every ABI are build products, not source. None are tracked:
# they were what exhausted the repo's LFS quota and took CI down. Each is
# reproduced by scripts/build-v8-<os>.sh, described by the committed
# component-manifest.json beside it, and fetched in CI by
# scripts/fetch-v8-archives.sh, which verifies the sha256 that manifest records.
engine/third_party/rusty_v8/*/librusty_v8.a
engine/third_party/rusty_v8/x86_64-linux-gnu/librusty_v8.a

# The `gn_out/` layout `dev-test-host.sh` expects (obj/librusty_v8.a plus
# src_binding.rs). It is hard links to the archive above, so it costs no disk and
# is regenerated by the three commands in the handoff's bootstrap section -- but
# without this line a `git add -A` would try to commit 127 MB of it.
engine/third_party/rusty_v8/*/gn_out/

# Windows V8 build products of scripts/build-v8-windows.sh, same policy as the
# Linux archive above: the ~190 MB rusty_v8.lib, the DLL and its import library
# are build products and are not tracked. src_binding.rs is NOT one of them --
# Windows bindgen cannot produce a correct binding (see build-v8-windows.sh's
# comment), so the committed one is a required input the build script reuses,
# same as the Android and Linux bindings beside it.
engine/third_party/rusty_v8/*-pc-windows-msvc/rusty_v8.lib
engine/third_party/rusty_v8/*-pc-windows-msvc/rusty_v8.dll
engine/third_party/rusty_v8/*-pc-windows-msvc/rusty_v8.dll.lib

# The Debian bullseye sysroot tree scripts/fetch-linux-sysroot.sh unpacks from
# Chromium's published tarball. sysroots.json beside it is tracked, and is the whole
# reason the tree is reproducible anywhere: it names the tarball and its sha256, and
# its own sha256 is the sysroot identity every Linux package manifest records.
engine/third_party/linux-sysroot/*
!engine/third_party/linux-sysroot/sysroots.json

# ANGLE + d3dcompiler_47 runtime DLLs scripts/fetch-windows-angle.sh installs,
# pinned by contracts/artifact-manifest/windows-angle.lock.json (which is
# tracked). No sibling file here plays sysroots.json's role because there is
# nothing to unpack -- each DLL is fetched and hash-checked individually.
# angle-windows-{x64,arm64}/ (no trailing slash after the arch, unlike the
# unsuffixed one above) are scripts/build-angle-windows.sh's own local build
# output -- the same class of build product, gitignored for the same reason,
# before it becomes a hash-pinned, fetchable release asset the way the
# unsuffixed directory's contents already are.
engine/third_party/angle-windows/
engine/third_party/angle-windows-*/

# Binaries built by the out-of-tree consumer examples. They are proof the
# package works, not source; tests/c_host/build-with-pkgconfig.sh and the
# CMake project regenerate them.
tests/c_host/linux/c-host
tests/c_host/linux/c-host-cmake

# Screenshots dropped in the repo root are scratch, not project content.
Snipaste_*.png

# Android example build outputs
tests/c_host/android/build/
tests/c_host/android/.cxx/
tests/c_host/android/src/main/jniLibs-static/
tests/c_host/linux/c-host

# Maintainer working notes, internal planning documents and one-off audit
# tooling. These are deliberately not published: they are the accumulated
# record of which trap was hit on which device and how each was resolved,
# which is the expensive half of this project to rebuild and is worth nothing
# to someone consuming the runtime. What a consumer needs is README.md,
# docs/BUILD.md, docs/multiplatform-architecture.md and include/migo/.
# Placed last so these win over the `!docs/` re-inclusion above.
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
GEMINI.md
.codex/
.superpowers/
docs/
platforms/windows/SPIKE-REPORT.md

# Local-only operational notes (`*.local.md`). Same policy as the working
# notes above: they are for the maintainer's machine, not for consumers.
*.local.md

# OpenHarmony host build inputs staged by scripts/build-ohos-host.sh
platforms/openharmony/entry/src/main/cpp/libs/
platforms/openharmony/entry/src/main/cpp/migo-include/

# Records of a third party's product -- not tooling, data. A dump of another
# runtime's global belongs to that runtime, so it stays out of a repository
# whose sources are published.
#
# The line is drawn at the data, not at the tool. It used to sit at the tool:
# scripts/prescreen-game.sh and its fixture were ignored wholesale because they
# mention `wx`. But that scanner carries no third-party knowledge -- it reads a
# bundle's identifiers and compares them against **this build's own** published
# surface, with `wx` appearing only as a literal identifier, which is exactly
# what the change that introduced this rule said it would keep. Ignoring it also
# put it beyond CI, so nothing could hold it to a contract; the three ways it
# over-reported gaps were found by hand on real bundles rather than by a gate.
# The reference table below is the part that is genuinely someone else's.
tools/wx-api-diff/
scripts/perf_compare.ps1
